"Summarizes distributions with a median, quartile box, IQR-based whiskers, and separately marked outside observations.\n\nUse when: Several groups need compact comparisons of center and spread. The underlying sample is large enough for stable quartile summaries.\n\nAvoid when: Multimodality or small-sample raw values are central to the question.\n\nVisual grammar: Box endpoints encode the first and third quartiles, with an internal median line. Whiskers reach the most extreme observations within 1.5 IQR fences; remaining points are drawn separately.\n\nBuild: Calculate quartiles using a stated convention. Find the furthest observed values within the lower and upper fences. Draw boxes, whiskers, outside points, and group sample sizes.\n\nCheck: Check whiskers end at observations rather than the theoretical fences. Verify outside values are retained and not treated as errors automatically. Confirm common numeric scales and quartile conventions across groups.\n\nPitfalls: A box hides multimodality and detailed tail shape. Whiskers do not encode confidence intervals or guaranteed min/max values.\n\nExample: Compare synthetic repair times by device family with Tukey whiskers and every outside observation retained.\n\nAlternatives: Min–max box plot, Violin plot, Raincloud plot."
